      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "779b021c-5cca-54eb-b867-17b640d742d2",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Events: Glorious Revolution\n- Laws: Church Patronage (Scotland) Act 1711\n- Organizations: Church of Scotland\n- Roles: Patrons, English Parliamentarians\n- People: James II of England, James VII of Scotland, William III, Prince of Orange\n\nFacts:\n- The Glorious Revolution, also called the Revolution of 1688, overthrew King James II of England, who was also James VII of Scotland.\n- The Glorious Revolution was carried out by a union of English Parliamentarians and the Dutch stadtholder William III, Prince of Orange.\n- During the Glorious Revolution, noble and other Patrons in Scotland lost their customary control over Church of Scotland parish churches.\n- The Church Patronage (Scotland) Act 1711 was intended to restore control over Church of Scotland parish churches to noble and other Patrons in Scotland.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "ebf81efb-a636-5857-bf57-10b90dba2af7",
        "text": "Page: Church Patronage (Scotland) Act 1711. Sentence: Its purpose was to allow the noble and other Patrons in Scotland to gain control over the Church of Scotland parish churches again, having lost that custom in the so-called Glorious Revolution.\nPage: Glorious Revolution. Sentence: The Glorious Revolution, also called the Revolution of 1688, was the overthrow of King James II of England (James VII of Scotland) by a union of English Parliamentarians with the Dutch stadtholder William III, Prince of Orange.",
